@startuml tunneling
' How device B (the SelfPrivacy backend) is reached over HTTPS, AND where the public name comes from.
' The hard part: B sits behind NAT (often CGNAT / a third party's router), so there is usually no
' inbound port to forward. These are the four routes, how each scores, and the name each one needs.
' Rendered deterministically to tunneling.svg by docs/render-diagrams.sh (pinned plantuml).
title Reaching the SelfPrivacy backend (device B) over HTTPS — routes & where the name comes from

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam ArrowColor #444444
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #555555
  roundCorner 10
}

left to right direction

rectangle "**Visitor**\nphone / laptop,\nanywhere" as V
rectangle "**device B**\nSelfPrivacy backend :443\n(Host-routed vhosts)" as B #EEF3FB

rectangle "**Tunnel relay**  (B dials OUT — centralised, CGNAT-proof)" as T #F7E7A6 {
  rectangle "**Tailscale** Funnel\nstable *.ts.net, valid cert\n(recommended default)" as T_TS #FFF3C4
  rectangle "**Cloudflare**\nquick *.trycloudflare.com\nOR named (your domain, all 5)" as T_CF #FFF3C4
  rectangle "**ngrok**\n*.ngrok-free.app" as T_NG #FFF3C4
  rectangle "**Pinggy**\n*.pinggy.link (~60 min free)" as T_PG #FFF3C4
  rectangle "**LocalTunnel**\n*.loca.lt" as T_LT #FFF3C4
}
' DuckDNS is NOT a tunnel — it is dynamic DNS that publishes an AAAA at the box's OWN public IPv6;
' the visitor connects DIRECTLY (no relay). That is why it is decentralised (score D), unlike the relays.
rectangle "**DuckDNS** (free) — direct IPv6, NOT a tunnel\ngives you  name.duckdns.org\n(no domain to buy)\nsets AAAA → B's own IPv6" as DD #CDEBC5
rectangle "**Registrar / DNS host**\ne.g. theory7\nA / AAAA / NS" as REG
rectangle "**Router R**\nforward :443" as R

V --> T  : https
T ..> B  : outbound tunnel\n(centralised: via relay)

V --> DD : https (IPv6)
DD --> B : AAAA → B's IPv6\n(no NAT; open firewall)

V --> REG : https (IPv4)
REG --> R : **A** → public IPv4
R --> B   : port-forward :443

V --> B  : **.onion** via Tor

note bottom of B
  **Requirement — 5 names.** SelfPrivacy serves 5 vhosts on :443, routed by Host header, so
  each service needs a public name:  **api.**  **cloud.**  **git.**  **matrix.**  **meet.**<domain>
  (one wildcard *.<domain>, or 5 DNS records / 5 tunnel public-hostnames).
end note

rectangle "**FREE domain sources** (when you must bring your OWN name)\n**nic.eu.org** — delegable NS → the ONLY free source that works with a **Cloudflare named tunnel** (<color:orange>slow manual approval</color>)\n**DuckDNS** / **freedns.afraid.org** — set the IP only (A/AAAA), no NS delegation → **direct-IPv6** or **port-forward**, NOT a Cloudflare tunnel\n(a paid registrar like **theory7** works for all routes)" as SRC #E8F5E9
SRC ..> T   : eu.org
SRC ..> REG : paid

legend bottom
<b>Scores</b>   A safe · B reliable across reboot/IP-change · C free · D decentralised · E human-readable name
<back:#CDEBC5>    </back> <b>direct IPv6 + DuckDNS</b> — A B C <b>D</b> E · no relay/port-forward · but needs public IPv6 on BOTH the box AND the visitor (~half of EU; low in .nl)
<back:#F7E7A6>    </back> <b>tunnel relay</b> — A B C · (E only with your OWN domain; free-host = random name) · behind-CGNAT, but NOT D; CF ToS caps video
<back:#FFFFFF>    </back> <b>port-forward IPv4</b> — A B C D E · needs router admin + a real public IPv4 (dead under CGNAT)
<b>.onion (Tor)</b> — A B C D · always works, no infra · but not E (56-char) and Tor-only
endlegend
@enduml
